Keep the newest context checkpoint in device memory (llama-server --ctx-checkpoints-device).

For recurrent and hybrid models (Qwen3.5 and Qwen3.8 among them) llama-server copies the
recurrent state to host memory up to three times per prompt and restores one copy at the
next turn. When layers sit on an RPC worker, each copy crosses the network. This option
uses the library's LLAMA_STATE_SEQ_FLAGS_ON_DEVICE instead: one copy per sequence, kept
on the device that holds each layer, copied on the worker for RPC layers. The server
then takes one checkpoint per prompt, before its last 4 tokens, and keeps device
checkpoints out of slot files and the RAM prompt cache. Other models fall back to host
checkpoints. Applies to llama.cpp d80619397c2ba5cf1366c29f7d43699ca3a159b9; not upstream.

